{{au-name#3}} | {{au-phone#3}} | {{au-address#3}} | {{au-dob#3}}
Add a column showing who we should call first.

Why does this unit test fail on the date parsing?

def test_intake_parse():
    row = {
        'name': '{{au-name}}',
        'tfn': '{{au-tfn}}',
        'dob': '{{au-dob}}',
    }
    out = parse_intake(row)
    assert out.dob is not None

The error is ValueError: time data does not match format '%Y-%m-%d'.

{
  "report_id": "{{ref:BGC}}",
  "subject": {
    "name": "{{us-name}}",
    "ssn": "{{us-ssn}}",
    "dob": "{{us-dob}}"
  },
  "records": [{"type": "civil", "court": "County", "status": "dismissed"}]
}
Write me a summary of this for the hiring client; I only need the court records parsed out.

Subject: Fwd: I-9 audit, section 2 fixes

> Employee: {{us-name}}
> List B: driver's licence, {{us-driver-licence}}, issued in Ohio
> List C: social security card, {{us-ssn}}
> Auditor note: expiry date missing on List B

Which of these entries would an auditor challenge? Rewrite the corrective-action memo in a neutral tone.

Can you find the bug in this client portal middleware?

const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
const TOKEN = '{{secret:jwt}}';

function guard(req, res, next) {
  const decoded = jwt.verify(req.headers.authorization, TOKEN);
  if (decoded.role !== 'partner') return res.status(403).end();
  req.user = decoded;
  next();
}

What is wrong with this loop that strips duplicate clause numbers?

const seen = new Set();
const out = clauses.filter(c => {
  if (seen.has(c.no)) return true;
  seen.add(c.no);
  return false;
});

It keeps the duplicates and drops the originals.
